WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.030
The report ran perfectly yesterday.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.610
Tonight the scheduled job fails, and a colleague gets different numbers from the same script.

3
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.990
Nothing in the code changed.

4
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.690
Something in the environment did.

5
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:21.610
This world is about the invisible state your code depends on: which packages are attached, which variables a function can see, and how the session is configured.

6
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:30.700
When you call a function, R looks for its name along the search path: your global environment first, then every attached package, newest first.

7
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.000
Attach MASS after dplyr, and MASS sits in front.

8
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.180
Its select() now wins, and the error message, "unused arguments", says nothing about why.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.650
So diagnose before you guess.

10
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.410
search() shows the order.

11
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.850
environment(select) tells you which package owns the version you are calling.

12
00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.230
conflicts() lists every name defined more than once.

13
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.770
Here, the culprit is clear: select lives in two places, and MASS is in front.

14
00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.240
The robust fix is to stop depending on the order.

15
00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.700
Inside functions, write dplyr::select(), and the same for filter().

16
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.640
Qualified calls work whatever is attached, even when dplyr is not attached at all.

17
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.540
Without it, a bare filter() quietly means the time series filter from stats.

18
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.880
A second kind of bug hides inside functions.

19
00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:25.020
When a function uses a name it was not given, R looks it up where the function was defined, usually the global environment.

20
00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:33.960
flag_signals() compares p-values with a global threshold, so changing that variable silently changes every result, and its alpha argument is ignored.

21
00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.200
You can find these free variables automatically.

22
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.780
codetools::findGlobals() reports that the function reads threshold.

23
00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:47.120
The fix is a pure function: everything it needs arrives through its arguments, with sensible defaults.

24
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.400
Now alpha decides, and nothing outside the function can change the answer.

25
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.770
Next, the session itself.

26
00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:03.770
A relative path, like "visits.csv", is resolved against the working directory, and that differs between your laptop, a scheduled job, and a colleague's machine.

27
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:09.010
Build the path from an argument with file.path(), so the caller decides where the data lives.

28
00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.100
Global options change output too.

29
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:19.380
format() follows options(OutDec), so a session set up for a decimal comma prints "10,39".

30
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.780
sprintf() always uses a decimal point.

31
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:27.060
For numbers that end up in reports, prefer formatting that does not depend on the session.

32
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:32.300
If a function must change global state, it should put things back.

33
00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:37.660
Save the old value and restore it with on.exit(), which runs however the function ends, even after an error.

34
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:40.120
The withr package wraps the same pattern.

35
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.880
A well-behaved function leaves the session exactly as it found it.

36
00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.920
Finally, record what the result ran on.

37
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:55.000
The R version, the version of each package you used, and a checksum of the input file let anyone confirm they are reproducing the same thing.

38
00:02:55.510 --> 00:03:00.780
On your own machine, the renv package goes further and pins exact package versions in a lockfile.

39
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:05.130
Your Debugging Crisis missions follow this path.

40
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.830
Unmask a broken pipeline.

41
00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.530
Make a scoping bug impossible.

42
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:17.610
Then face the boss: a report that must give identical results from any directory, under any options, with or without dplyr attached, while recording its own provenance.
